1
00:00:25,592 --> 00:00:28,228
- Hey!
- What?

2
00:00:28,294 --> 00:00:30,296
- Let's have barbecue after this.
- Okay.

3
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:31,231
Special spareribs.

4
00:00:31,297 --> 00:00:32,465
I said okay.

5
00:00:34,567 --> 00:00:35,769
Where should we go?

6
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:08,134
Can you believe it?

7
00:01:08,501 --> 00:01:11,438
He can't hear.

8
00:01:14,941 --> 00:01:17,744
DEAF PIANIST TAKASHI NANAO

9
00:01:23,716 --> 00:01:27,220
He cannot hear the notes he is performing.

10
00:01:27,587 --> 00:01:28,855
Can you believe it?

11
00:01:28,922 --> 00:01:30,023
Be quiet!

12
00:01:30,957 --> 00:01:31,791
See?

13
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:07,894
Takashi Nanao. He is a marvel.

14
00:02:07,961 --> 00:02:10,497
Surely an even more
marvellous performance.

15
00:02:10,563 --> 00:02:12,932
Michiko, how did you feel?
You felt something, right?

16
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:15,034
- I want spareribs!
- That again?

17
00:02:15,101 --> 00:02:17,103
- Excuse me...
- How unsophisticated...

18
00:02:18,538 --> 00:02:22,509
- Are you from Medical Referral?
- Yes, that is so.

19
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:25,612
Oh, great. You came.

20
00:02:26,379 --> 00:02:29,883
- Oh! Are you the one... who sent this?
- Yes.

21
00:02:29,949 --> 00:02:32,919
There's neither your name nor the address.

22
00:02:32,986 --> 00:02:35,655
To be honest, I found it so suspicious.

23
00:02:35,722 --> 00:02:38,892
Well, I am Mr. Nanao's assistant.

24
00:02:39,626 --> 00:02:42,028
I would like to introduce you to him.

25
00:02:42,095 --> 00:02:44,531
Are you sure? Thank you very much!

26
00:02:44,597 --> 00:02:46,199
- Michiko, wait here.
- The spareribs?

27
00:02:46,266 --> 00:02:48,301
- Excuse her.
- Let's go eat.

28
00:02:48,368 --> 00:02:49,736
Who cares about the spareribs?

29
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:54,307
"Takashi Nanao.
He was a 19-year-old college student

30
00:02:54,374 --> 00:02:59,145
with a rosy future promised.
Due to his pneumonitis,

31
00:02:59,612 --> 00:03:02,048
he lost hearing in both of his ears."

32
00:03:02,682 --> 00:03:05,151
It might be acquired
sensorineural hearing loss.

33
00:03:05,218 --> 00:03:08,321
"It was believed to be the end...

34
00:03:08,388 --> 00:03:09,956
of his career as a pianist,

35
00:03:10,023 --> 00:03:13,760
but he made a comeback to the stage

36
00:03:13,826 --> 00:03:16,129
with fascinating performance
which himself can't hear.

37
00:03:16,196 --> 00:03:19,499
Currently, he performs worldwide."

38
00:03:19,566 --> 00:03:20,400
That's amazing.

39
00:03:21,034 --> 00:03:22,368
Thanks for waiting

40
00:03:22,835 --> 00:03:25,338
there were so many reporters.

41
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:28,942
It was an amazing performance!

42
00:03:29,008 --> 00:03:30,243
Bravo!

43
00:03:31,844 --> 00:03:32,946
Thank you.

44
00:03:33,913 --> 00:03:34,847
You can hear us.

45
00:03:35,915 --> 00:03:38,952
I read people's lips.

46
00:03:39,686 --> 00:03:41,955
I read the lips to understand
what you are saying.

47
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:46,960
In my case, I wasn't born deaf,

48
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:49,295
so my pronunciation is still ok.

49
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:52,899
My assistant, Yuka, also helps me a lot.

50
00:03:53,566 --> 00:03:55,435
I assist him with sign language.

51
00:03:56,202 --> 00:03:59,439
Even so, being able to come this far...

52
00:03:59,505 --> 00:04:01,975
must have taken extreme effort
on your part.

53
00:04:02,909 --> 00:04:03,743
Please, have a seat.

54
00:04:04,877 --> 00:04:06,446
Thank you.

55
00:04:07,814 --> 00:04:14,487
I know which notes I should hit and how to
hit them to make the sound I want.

56
00:04:14,554 --> 00:04:16,756
Although I cannot hear,

57
00:04:17,156 --> 00:04:20,593
certainly, there is sound ringing
in my head.

58
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:22,729
Allow me to ask you something.

59
00:04:23,296 --> 00:04:27,233
In case you make a mistake
or hit the wrong notes,

60
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:30,637
is the sound in your head wrong as well?

61
00:04:30,703 --> 00:04:33,072
Of course. However,

62
00:04:35,275 --> 00:04:36,743
I never make mistakes.

63
00:04:41,547 --> 00:04:44,150
By the way, Yuka,
please introduce them to me.

64
00:04:45,652 --> 00:04:46,819
She is a doctor.

65
00:04:48,821 --> 00:04:51,758
Can I trouble you to autograph here?

66
00:04:51,824 --> 00:04:54,727
Please cure Mr. Nanao's hearing issue.

67
00:04:54,794 --> 00:04:55,962
Pardon?

68
00:04:56,796 --> 00:04:59,332
I did a lot of research.

69
00:04:59,399 --> 00:05:02,835
The doctors at this hospital
have taken in other patients...

70
00:05:02,902 --> 00:05:07,206
who other hospitals had turned down.

71
00:05:07,273 --> 00:05:09,108
You must have researched a lot.

72
00:05:09,175 --> 00:05:12,278
I got it. I will perform the operation.

73
00:05:13,179 --> 00:05:14,113
Really?

74
00:05:14,681 --> 00:05:15,615
You are...

75
00:05:15,682 --> 00:05:18,084
Freelance surgeon, Michiko Daimon.

76
00:05:18,951 --> 00:05:19,986
Dr. Daimon.

77
00:05:23,156 --> 00:05:24,290
What are you doing?

78
00:05:25,692 --> 00:05:27,260
Who are these people?

79
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:28,361
Oh, I am...

80
00:05:28,428 --> 00:05:31,364
- from Kanbara...
- You can't just come here...

81
00:05:32,498 --> 00:05:33,566
Here's my autograph.

82
00:05:37,503 --> 00:05:40,373
- Thank you very much for coming today.
- Thank you.

83
00:05:41,274 --> 00:05:42,108
Thank you.

84
00:05:43,509 --> 00:05:44,344
Excuse me.

85
00:05:54,921 --> 00:05:55,855
Bravo!

86
00:05:56,589 --> 00:05:59,559
Michiko, I will feed you as much
special spareribs as you'd like!

87
00:06:00,326 --> 00:06:01,661
- Really?
- Really!

88
00:06:01,728 --> 00:06:02,562
Bravo!

89
00:06:03,663 --> 00:06:06,799
This is the story of
a lone wolf woman doctor.

90
00:06:08,267 --> 00:06:09,669
<i>In 2016,</i>

91
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:12,538
<i>the once sacrosanct university</i>
<i>medical system was in crisis.</i>

92
00:06:13,206 --> 00:06:15,375
<i>The system on which lives depend</i>

93
00:06:15,441 --> 00:06:17,477
<i>had strayed away from what it should be.</i>

94
00:06:17,543 --> 00:06:19,746
The internal medicine
doctors are coming this way.

95
00:06:20,213 --> 00:06:22,448
We, surgeons, make our own path.

96
00:06:22,515 --> 00:06:23,549
- Yes sir!
- Yes sir!

97
00:06:23,616 --> 00:06:26,219
We are going to run straight
into them at this rate.

98
00:06:26,285 --> 00:06:29,489
All paths lead to internal medicine!

99
00:06:29,989 --> 00:06:30,823
- Yes sir!
- Yes sir!

100
00:06:31,224 --> 00:06:32,992
<i>Famous hospitals use...</i>

101
00:06:33,493 --> 00:06:35,728
<i>their brand power to push into the world.</i>

102
00:06:36,696 --> 00:06:39,298
<i>On the other hand,</i>
<i>highly skilled surgeons...</i>

103
00:06:39,766 --> 00:06:43,503
<i>in pursuit of a higher salary</i>
<i>were relocating to foreign countries.</i>

104
00:06:43,569 --> 00:06:46,472
- Move forward!
- Yes sir!

105
00:06:46,539 --> 00:06:48,207
We never retreat!

106
00:06:48,541 --> 00:06:49,375
- Yes sir!
- Yes sir!

107
00:06:49,942 --> 00:06:52,912
<i>The world of medicine has entered...</i>

108
00:06:52,979 --> 00:06:55,415
<i>into the global world</i>

109
00:06:55,815 --> 00:06:56,983
<i>of survival of the fittest.</i>

110
00:06:59,252 --> 00:07:00,353
<i>In the midst of it,</i>

111
00:07:00,420 --> 00:07:03,689
<i>a new breed of doctor has emerged.</i>
<i>Freelance doctors,</i>

112
00:07:03,756 --> 00:07:07,026
<i>in other words, lone doctors.</i>

113
00:07:15,635 --> 00:07:17,537
<i>This woman, for example.</i>

114
00:07:18,204 --> 00:07:20,973
<i>Despising the herd mentality, authority</i>

115
00:07:21,641 --> 00:07:23,075
<i>and institutional restraints...</i>

116
00:07:23,709 --> 00:07:26,712
<i>Her solo weapons are her license</i>

117
00:07:27,313 --> 00:07:28,781
<i>and the skills she has earned.</i>

118
00:07:33,586 --> 00:07:35,822
<i>Surgeon Michiko Daimon.</i>

119
00:07:36,422 --> 00:07:37,423
<i>Also known as...</i>

120
00:07:37,990 --> 00:07:39,459
<i>Doctor-X.</i>

121
00:08:08,554 --> 00:08:11,157
What in the hell am I doing here?

122
00:08:11,224 --> 00:08:12,291
What happened?

123
00:08:12,358 --> 00:08:13,292
In America,

124
00:08:13,359 --> 00:08:15,695
my value was highly recognised!

125
00:08:16,429 --> 00:08:17,430
My personal ability...

126
00:08:17,497 --> 00:08:20,466
was properly valued there.

127
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:23,870
I didn't come to this hospital
just to work in rotation.

128
00:08:23,936 --> 00:08:24,804
In America?

129
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:26,072
Yes, in America.

130
00:08:26,138 --> 00:08:27,707
This is Japan.

131
00:08:28,741 --> 00:08:29,842
What should we do?

132
00:08:29,909 --> 00:08:32,311
Kitano...

133
00:08:32,712 --> 00:08:34,013
- Please wait!
- Kitano!

134
00:08:34,080 --> 00:08:35,381
What is it, Chief Saionji?

135
00:08:35,448 --> 00:08:36,616
You know why I'm here.

136
00:08:36,983 --> 00:08:41,654
So, you have something to say
about the operation rotation?

137
00:08:41,721 --> 00:08:43,055
That's why I'm here
talking to Director Hiruma.

138
00:08:43,122 --> 00:08:46,359
Before you talk to the Director,
shouldn't you talk to me?

139
00:08:46,926 --> 00:08:51,230
Why do you go over your Chief's head
and talk straight to Director?

140
00:08:51,297 --> 00:08:53,599
That complicated procedure is
useless. It's more logical this way.

141
00:08:53,666 --> 00:08:56,068
That's an American mind.

142
00:08:56,502 --> 00:08:59,105
Director, this is Japan!

143
00:08:59,705 --> 00:09:01,407
That's exactly what I just said.

144
00:09:01,474 --> 00:09:03,910
I apologise for interrupting you.

145
00:09:04,277 --> 00:09:07,213
I'm not quite sure what
you're in the middle of.

146
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:08,848
Make an appointment first.

147
00:09:08,915 --> 00:09:13,019
- Let's just skip such useless formality.
- Wait...

148
00:09:14,153 --> 00:09:17,757
Being able to adapt
to any circumstances...

149
00:09:17,823 --> 00:09:19,926
is the good thing about Japanese culture.

150
00:09:20,393 --> 00:09:22,061
- So?
- What is that?

151
00:09:23,663 --> 00:09:27,667
Although this isn't something...

152
00:09:28,568 --> 00:09:30,736
I would normally do,

153
00:09:31,170 --> 00:09:34,173
- I would like to bring you a patient.
- A patient?

154
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:35,341
To us?

155
00:09:36,809 --> 00:09:38,811
"Deaf pianist..."

156
00:09:39,245 --> 00:09:41,547
Takashi Nanao! He's world famous!

157
00:09:41,614 --> 00:09:44,150
- World famous?
- Really?

158
00:09:44,216 --> 00:09:48,120
I'd like you to take on the surgery
to cure the hearing loss

159
00:09:48,187 --> 00:09:49,922
of this world-wide celebrity.

160
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:53,125
If the operation is a success,

161
00:09:53,526 --> 00:09:58,331
Toutei University Hospital
will also earn the world-wide fame.

162
00:09:59,198 --> 00:10:00,900
That is wonderful.

163
00:10:00,967 --> 00:10:07,139
How does it sound to have the world-known
Dr. Kitano perform the surgery?

164
00:10:07,206 --> 00:10:08,808
- That is no good.
- What?

165
00:10:09,442 --> 00:10:15,014
This is a personal offer directly
requested to Kanbara Medical Referral.

166
00:10:15,081 --> 00:10:18,417
Therefore, Michiko Daimon...

167
00:10:18,484 --> 00:10:21,287
will perform the operation
in your hospital.

168
00:10:21,354 --> 00:10:22,989
- No.
- Sorry?

169
00:10:23,389 --> 00:10:27,693
The eyes of the whole world
will be centred on this operation.

170
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:32,698
If I were to let a freelancer
like Michiko Daimon perform the surgery,

171
00:10:32,765 --> 00:10:34,934
it'd be such a disgrace.

172
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:37,069
- Yes sir.
- That again?

173
00:10:37,136 --> 00:10:39,238
Yes, that again. Do you have a problem?

174
00:10:39,305 --> 00:10:40,239
In that case,

175
00:10:40,673 --> 00:10:43,809
I will take this case to another hospital.

176
00:10:44,944 --> 00:10:45,811
What?

177
00:10:47,747 --> 00:10:49,048
Give it back. Gosh.

178
00:10:52,084 --> 00:10:53,085
Just... Wait...

179
00:10:53,586 --> 00:10:56,088
Well, then how about this?

180
00:10:56,856 --> 00:11:00,559
I will buy this patient.

181
00:11:01,994 --> 00:11:03,663
At the price you want.

182
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:06,065
- At the price I suggest?
- Yes.

183
00:11:07,366 --> 00:11:08,367
Isn't it a great deal?

184
00:11:12,405 --> 00:11:14,006
Thank you.

185
00:11:14,073 --> 00:11:15,741
No, it's my pleasure.

186
00:11:16,342 --> 00:11:19,211
About the melon,

187
00:11:19,545 --> 00:11:21,247
it will not be necessary this time.

188
00:11:21,313 --> 00:11:23,115
I understand.

189
00:11:25,851 --> 00:11:28,921
He'll pay me as much as I want.
This is great!

190
00:11:31,057 --> 00:11:32,858
- What?
- It's so regrettable.

191
00:11:32,925 --> 00:11:36,195
It really is, I can't believe it!

192
00:11:36,262 --> 00:11:37,997
So I can't do the surgery?

193
00:11:38,064 --> 00:11:40,833
Director Hiruma brought up...

194
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:44,203
strange University Hospital
rules and regulations.

195
00:11:44,670 --> 00:11:46,972
He said he is going
to have Dr. Kitano do it.

196
00:11:48,074 --> 00:11:50,309
How disappointing.

197
00:11:50,376 --> 00:11:54,080
In that case, you should have just sold
the patient to Hiruma at a high price.

198
00:11:54,146 --> 00:11:55,214
What?

199
00:11:55,281 --> 00:11:58,984
Hiromi, you know Akira
wouldn't do something like that.

200
00:11:59,051 --> 00:12:01,153
As the head of
Kanbara Locum Doctor Agency,

201
00:12:01,954 --> 00:12:06,025
he has the pride.
He'd never do such things.

202
00:12:06,092 --> 00:12:08,594
Oh, right. You do have pride, don't you?

203
00:12:08,661 --> 00:12:10,996
What a shame...

204
00:12:11,597 --> 00:12:12,631
I win.

205
00:12:13,265 --> 00:12:15,501
Michiko, what's the matter with you?

206
00:12:16,802 --> 00:12:19,071
I lined all sorts up here. Look.

207
00:12:19,672 --> 00:12:22,108
- What?
- I win...

208
00:12:41,494 --> 00:12:43,062
I am Mamoru Hara...

209
00:12:48,234 --> 00:12:49,535
from the same class as you.

210
00:12:50,336 --> 00:12:51,170
Why are you here?

211
00:12:52,171 --> 00:12:57,209
I have looked over your career history
from your time in America several times.

212
00:12:57,777 --> 00:13:00,446
There are many brilliant
medical cases listed.

213
00:13:01,013 --> 00:13:02,281
Thank you.

214
00:13:02,348 --> 00:13:06,619
However, you have never performed surgery
on a patient with hearing impairment,

215
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:09,054
correct?

216
00:13:09,955 --> 00:13:10,790
So?

217
00:13:12,925 --> 00:13:14,093
If you need,

218
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:19,198
I can ask a specialist
to come in to help you.

219
00:13:20,733 --> 00:13:22,401
I don't need your concern.

220
00:13:24,804 --> 00:13:28,440
I see. Well, I'm glad to hear that.

221
00:13:30,509 --> 00:13:32,144
Did you come here just to say that?

222
00:13:33,345 --> 00:13:34,380
Well...

223
00:13:35,648 --> 00:13:36,515
I'm sorry.

224
00:13:37,750 --> 00:13:38,717
No problem.

225
00:13:43,556 --> 00:13:45,457
Hey, just do it!

226
00:13:46,592 --> 00:13:47,493
Yes sir.

227
00:13:51,864 --> 00:13:52,698
Bastard!

228
00:14:01,140 --> 00:14:02,608
It's freezing!

229
00:14:04,643 --> 00:14:05,544
Wait,

230
00:14:06,312 --> 00:14:07,246
she is...

231
00:14:08,314 --> 00:14:09,448
Hey, what's wrong?

232
00:14:10,649 --> 00:14:11,984
Are you here to visit the hospital...

233
00:14:15,187 --> 00:14:16,322
Hey!

234
00:14:23,929 --> 00:14:25,664
Watch where you're going!

235
00:14:27,166 --> 00:14:29,168
I'm sorry, thank you very much.

236
00:14:29,602 --> 00:14:31,470
Oh, Dr. Daimon!

237
00:14:33,873 --> 00:14:35,774
Do you also have a hearing disorder?

238
00:14:39,478 --> 00:14:42,948
Actually, when I was in high school,

239
00:14:43,315 --> 00:14:45,084
I lost the hearing in my left ear
due to tympanitis.

240
00:14:45,551 --> 00:14:46,385
I see.

241
00:14:47,286 --> 00:14:51,156
But just now, I called out to you
from your back right.

242
00:14:53,659 --> 00:14:54,960
I have a cold.

243
00:14:56,428 --> 00:14:58,697
Mr. Nanao will have
an examination today, right?

244
00:14:58,764 --> 00:15:00,332
So I came here.

245
00:15:00,399 --> 00:15:02,301
Today is his examination?

246
00:15:02,368 --> 00:15:03,235
Sorry?

247
00:15:03,636 --> 00:15:05,838
I won't be performing
the operation anymore.

248
00:15:06,438 --> 00:15:07,273
What?

249
00:15:08,307 --> 00:15:09,241
Postponed?

250
00:15:09,308 --> 00:15:11,043
He said he's not available today.

251
00:15:12,344 --> 00:15:13,178
Please excuse me.

252
00:15:14,313 --> 00:15:15,781
Thank you.

253
00:15:16,882 --> 00:15:20,219
He must be off today.
Did something happen to him?

254
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:02,861
- You're good.
- Thanks.

255
00:16:03,862 --> 00:16:06,765
All right. One more time.

256
00:16:10,302 --> 00:16:12,871
I was in the table tennis club
until junior year of high school.

257
00:16:12,938 --> 00:16:14,606
That's why you're good.

258
00:16:15,507 --> 00:16:18,944
I was aiming for the Olympics.

259
00:16:20,946 --> 00:16:24,350
But...I've become able to hear
only on one side...

260
00:16:25,684 --> 00:16:28,320
Do you know that?
When you play table tennis,

261
00:16:28,387 --> 00:16:31,557
you can judge the opponent's hit
by the pitch of the ball.

262
00:16:33,325 --> 00:16:34,360
So,

263
00:16:35,894 --> 00:16:37,329
After I'd have hearing loss,

264
00:16:41,333 --> 00:16:42,201
I gave up.

265
00:16:42,768 --> 00:16:43,602
Oh...

266
00:16:44,169 --> 00:16:47,373
That's when I came across
Mr. Nanao's performance,

267
00:16:48,073 --> 00:16:49,375
and it cheered me up.

268
00:16:50,409 --> 00:16:53,078
Since then, I studied sign language...

269
00:16:53,145 --> 00:16:54,513
and became his assistant.

270
00:16:55,881 --> 00:16:56,949
I see.

271
00:17:00,085 --> 00:17:03,522
Why didn't he come to the examination?

272
00:17:05,090 --> 00:17:08,327
Well, they do say artists are fickle.

273
00:17:09,328 --> 00:17:11,230
I'm sure he's scared.

274
00:17:11,997 --> 00:17:12,831
Scared?

275
00:17:13,599 --> 00:17:16,068
I think he's scared of
the examination's results.

276
00:17:16,435 --> 00:17:20,639
He doesn't want to hear that
his hearing is not curable.

277
00:17:21,874 --> 00:17:25,210
I've encouraged him to get examined
several times before.

278
00:17:25,844 --> 00:17:26,712
But he always said no.

279
00:17:28,447 --> 00:17:30,816
He said that it is unnecessary.

280
00:17:32,117 --> 00:17:33,152
Unnecessary?

281
00:17:34,987 --> 00:17:36,822
If he regains his hearing,

282
00:17:37,523 --> 00:17:41,560
I'm sure he would be an even more
amazing pianist than he is now.

283
00:17:42,594 --> 00:17:46,198
I'd love to hear his musical performance.

284
00:17:49,735 --> 00:17:53,872
Let's play one more match!
Take it easier on me this time.

285
00:17:53,939 --> 00:17:54,773
Okay.

286
00:17:59,411 --> 00:18:00,479
What's wrong?

287
00:18:02,114 --> 00:18:05,050
I haven't played for a long time,
so I guess I'm tired.

288
00:18:05,851 --> 00:18:06,885
I've caught a cold as well.

289
00:18:10,189 --> 00:18:13,759
- Michiko, are you still not finished?
- It's boring with just the three of us.

290
00:18:13,826 --> 00:18:15,060
Yes! I win!

291
00:18:15,127 --> 00:18:16,361
See?

292
00:18:17,563 --> 00:18:18,764
Michiko.

293
00:18:19,598 --> 00:18:20,432
Hey.

294
00:18:21,834 --> 00:18:23,402
A 22-year-old patient...

295
00:18:23,469 --> 00:18:27,573
lost hearing in her left ear at the age
of 16 due to a case of tympanitis.

296
00:18:28,173 --> 00:18:30,909
And she now experiences
hearing loss in her right ear.

297
00:18:30,976 --> 00:18:32,811
She says it's because of a cold.

298
00:18:32,878 --> 00:18:34,446
Couldn't the virus have caused it?

299
00:18:35,347 --> 00:18:39,151
It's possible,
but what I can't get out of my head is...

300
00:18:43,388 --> 00:18:44,723
the facial pain.

301
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:46,125
Do you mean trigeminal neuralgia?

302
00:19:02,875 --> 00:19:05,010
Sir, are you alright?

303
00:19:07,279 --> 00:19:10,349
Thank you, Yuka.
I can handle the rest alone.

304
00:19:12,417 --> 00:19:13,252
But...

305
00:19:14,386 --> 00:19:15,320
I'll be fine.

306
00:19:17,589 --> 00:19:20,659
Mr. Nanao, we've been waiting for you.
Come this way.

307
00:19:33,839 --> 00:19:34,706
Dr. Kitano.

308
00:19:35,374 --> 00:19:36,808
Dr. Kitano.

309
00:19:37,209 --> 00:19:38,143
What?

310
00:19:38,610 --> 00:19:40,012
What's the matter?

311
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:45,717
Well...While I was working
at a hospital in Hokkaido,

312
00:19:45,784 --> 00:19:51,356
I once participated
in an ossiphone placement surgery.

313
00:19:52,724 --> 00:19:54,326
I saved all the records in this.

314
00:19:56,495 --> 00:19:58,730
If you'd like to use it
just as a reference, please.

315
00:20:04,136 --> 00:20:07,472
Well...Thank you for your time.

316
00:20:11,677 --> 00:20:15,180
Hey, that pianist is here, isn't he?

317
00:20:15,247 --> 00:20:17,482
He will be in the special
reception office in 30 minutes.

318
00:20:18,617 --> 00:20:19,451
What?

319
00:20:22,054 --> 00:20:24,022
SURGERY ON MIDDLE EAR AND
TEMPORAL BONE AND IMAGE DIAGNOSIS

320
00:20:24,089 --> 00:20:27,893
That is unusual for you.

321
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:28,794
What is?

322
00:20:28,860 --> 00:20:30,562
You'll assist Dr. Kitano
during the operation, right?

323
00:20:31,964 --> 00:20:35,500
Is that so? Well, if you insist...

324
00:20:35,567 --> 00:20:36,602
I decline.

325
00:20:39,671 --> 00:20:40,539
Do it.

326
00:20:44,142 --> 00:20:45,677
Excuse me.

327
00:20:50,616 --> 00:20:52,818
Ouch. That hurts! What is it? It hurts.

328
00:20:57,222 --> 00:20:58,924
"Special reception room."

329
00:21:00,459 --> 00:21:01,627
Here it is.

330
00:21:01,693 --> 00:21:02,594
SPECIAL RECEPTION ROOM

331
00:21:03,161 --> 00:21:04,029
Thank you for coming.

332
00:21:04,663 --> 00:21:05,697
I am Kitano.

333
00:21:07,332 --> 00:21:09,935
We have arranged an interpreter for you.

334
00:21:10,535 --> 00:21:12,170
If you need her, please let us know.

335
00:21:13,472 --> 00:21:14,306
Thanks.

336
00:21:14,706 --> 00:21:16,575
This is Hara. He will be...

337
00:21:16,642 --> 00:21:20,012
assisting me during the operation.

338
00:21:20,445 --> 00:21:21,280
Sorry?

339
00:21:24,516 --> 00:21:25,517
It's a pleasure to meet you.

340
00:21:27,019 --> 00:21:29,488
I heard you worked at
an American hospital.

341
00:21:31,023 --> 00:21:33,425
Actually, when I was in my 20s,

342
00:21:33,792 --> 00:21:36,895
I went to St Kevin's Medical Centre
in Illinois...

343
00:21:36,962 --> 00:21:39,197
for a consultation about my ears.

344
00:21:41,300 --> 00:21:46,405
Well...You understand quite well
just by reading lips.

345
00:21:46,471 --> 00:21:47,306
Yes.

346
00:21:48,373 --> 00:21:50,676
Do you know Professor Tranklin?

347
00:21:52,744 --> 00:21:55,280
I have met him several times
at academic meetings.

348
00:21:56,281 --> 00:21:57,616
I think.

349
00:21:57,683 --> 00:22:01,420
Professor Tranklin has a world authority
on hearing disorders.

350
00:22:02,020 --> 00:22:06,258
Still, he said there is a risk
that comes along with the surgery.

351
00:22:07,993 --> 00:22:08,860
Is that so?

352
00:22:09,594 --> 00:22:13,098
Dr. Kitano, what method of surgery
are you thinking of performing?

353
00:22:14,399 --> 00:22:15,834
Well...

354
00:22:18,870 --> 00:22:21,206
In the case of 90 dB degree hearing loss,

355
00:22:21,273 --> 00:22:23,608
first we will embed cochlea implants
in place.

356
00:22:25,844 --> 00:22:28,780
Doctor. You should talk a little slower.

357
00:22:30,749 --> 00:22:31,616
I apologise.

358
00:22:32,951 --> 00:22:35,187
- In case of...
- Cochlea implants?

359
00:22:36,521 --> 00:22:37,489
Yes.

360
00:22:38,523 --> 00:22:42,160
The surgery we suggest is placement
of newly developed...

361
00:22:42,227 --> 00:22:45,330
120-channel cochlear implant.

362
00:22:49,501 --> 00:22:50,736
Did I talk too fast?

363
00:22:50,802 --> 00:22:52,170
You were too fast.

364
00:23:12,457 --> 00:23:13,492
Let's go get an examination.

365
00:23:15,293 --> 00:23:16,128
Pardon?

366
00:23:17,396 --> 00:23:19,030
Mr. Nanao is still inside...

367
00:23:19,097 --> 00:23:20,532
Your examination.

368
00:23:20,599 --> 00:23:21,933
Wait.

369
00:23:23,135 --> 00:23:25,937
You said you caught a cold,
and it was a lie.

370
00:23:52,264 --> 00:23:53,432
I knew it.

371
00:24:00,872 --> 00:24:01,873
I knew it.

372
00:24:06,478 --> 00:24:09,815
The result shows that in your case,

373
00:24:10,782 --> 00:24:14,920
the ossification of the inner ear
is quite advanced.

374
00:24:15,320 --> 00:24:19,024
So unfortunately,
that implants are unsuitable for you.

375
00:24:20,559 --> 00:24:21,426
I see.

376
00:24:22,093 --> 00:24:25,030
But...it's too early to give up.

377
00:24:26,965 --> 00:24:27,799
Actually,

378
00:24:28,266 --> 00:24:31,069
a hearing aid that's developed
very recently called...

379
00:24:31,136 --> 00:24:34,039
- auditory midbrain...
- Auditory midbrain implant?

380
00:24:34,439 --> 00:24:35,540
Have you heard of them?

381
00:24:35,607 --> 00:24:36,741
I heard that

382
00:24:36,808 --> 00:24:39,845
it's a ground-breaking invention.
It uses electricity to directly

383
00:24:39,911 --> 00:24:42,981
stimulate the midbrain
which is located deeper

384
00:24:43,048 --> 00:24:46,551
than the auditory nerves
to restore hearing ability.

385
00:24:46,885 --> 00:24:49,554
If you've heard of them,
then this will go faster.

386
00:24:53,091 --> 00:24:54,259
What is going on here?

387
00:24:54,326 --> 00:24:55,460
Yasuhara.

388
00:24:56,661 --> 00:25:00,632
I found Mr. Nanao
a little strange these days,

389
00:25:01,066 --> 00:25:03,902
so I had a staff member watch over him.

390
00:25:06,538 --> 00:25:07,472
Mr. Nanao,

391
00:25:08,039 --> 00:25:10,876
are you considering receiving surgery
on your ears?

392
00:25:13,011 --> 00:25:13,979
I oppose.

393
00:25:14,880 --> 00:25:16,381
I will not approve of the surgery.

394
00:25:16,948 --> 00:25:18,116
It's out of question.

395
00:25:18,183 --> 00:25:19,918
Hey, what is it that you really want?

396
00:25:19,985 --> 00:25:22,120
If you regain your hearing,

397
00:25:22,187 --> 00:25:24,656
your value as a pianist will bottom out.

398
00:25:24,723 --> 00:25:26,758
Still, you can't say that to...

399
00:25:26,825 --> 00:25:29,494
Do you get it? I might sound heartless,

400
00:25:30,362 --> 00:25:31,796
but you've gained such popularity...

401
00:25:32,430 --> 00:25:36,067
only because you can't hear anything.

402
00:25:36,134 --> 00:25:38,837
If you are able to hear again
and become a normal pianist,

403
00:25:38,904 --> 00:25:40,572
no one will pay attention to you anymore.

404
00:25:40,639 --> 00:25:42,908
That's enough! How dare you say...

405
00:25:42,974 --> 00:25:43,808
Yasuhara.

406
00:25:45,477 --> 00:25:46,578
It's okay.

407
00:25:47,579 --> 00:25:50,982
I...will not get the surgery.

408
00:25:52,050 --> 00:25:53,051
What?

409
00:25:56,187 --> 00:25:57,222
Excuse me.

410
00:26:06,331 --> 00:26:07,465
What is this?

411
00:26:08,567 --> 00:26:10,735
I had to go through trouble
because of them.

412
00:26:13,371 --> 00:26:16,308
Right. It must've been hard for you.

413
00:26:18,476 --> 00:26:20,078
No, that's not what I meant!

414
00:26:20,145 --> 00:26:24,049
What? Mr. Nanao did what?
He rejected the surgery?

415
00:26:24,115 --> 00:26:26,151
No, that is no good!

416
00:26:26,217 --> 00:26:27,485
That will cause us trouble.

417
00:26:27,552 --> 00:26:30,021
You have to do something!

418
00:26:30,088 --> 00:26:31,756
I'm sorry for making you worried.

419
00:26:34,225 --> 00:26:37,963
Well, I'll go pull off the car.

420
00:26:44,102 --> 00:26:45,036
Mr. Nanao!

421
00:26:45,937 --> 00:26:48,974
Mr. Nanao!

422
00:26:53,411 --> 00:26:54,613
The director wants to see you.

423
00:26:58,683 --> 00:27:02,120
I'm sorry...for stopping you.

424
00:27:03,788 --> 00:27:05,357
You came just in time.

425
00:27:05,724 --> 00:27:11,496
Well, Mr. Nanao.
I heard that you rejected...

426
00:27:11,563 --> 00:27:15,767
- our super doctor Dr. Kitano's operation.
- Yes.

427
00:27:15,834 --> 00:27:17,569
Was it because of your manager?

428
00:27:17,636 --> 00:27:20,905
Doctor, can you talk a little slower?

429
00:27:22,540 --> 00:27:26,077
If you get the surgery
and your hearing is restored,

430
00:27:26,144 --> 00:27:28,847
you will lose your commodity value.
To put it shortly,

431
00:27:28,913 --> 00:27:31,483
you will no longer make money.

432
00:27:31,549 --> 00:27:32,684
Stop it.

433
00:27:36,955 --> 00:27:41,926
I just don't want to undergo
midbrain implant surgery.

434
00:27:43,795 --> 00:27:45,296
If I receive that surgery,

435
00:27:46,231 --> 00:27:48,900
I might be able to freely
have conversations with people.

436
00:27:49,601 --> 00:27:53,838
But the raw sounds of performance and
instruments will sound artificial to me,

437
00:27:53,905 --> 00:27:54,873
making me hard to distinguish
different sounds.

438
00:27:55,607 --> 00:27:57,042
It is true that the frequency

439
00:27:57,709 --> 00:28:01,046
of sounds you hear will decrease.

440
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:02,847
However,

441
00:28:03,481 --> 00:28:07,185
in place of that, the inconvenience
you experience daily will disappear.

442
00:28:07,886 --> 00:28:10,221
Even this will be a world of difference.

443
00:28:11,690 --> 00:28:14,559
I am a musician.

444
00:28:16,628 --> 00:28:20,532
Even if my ears are restored
and I can hear in daily life,

445
00:28:21,299 --> 00:28:24,135
there is no point
if I cannot distinguish different sounds.

446
00:28:25,770 --> 00:28:27,972
To me, it is useless.

447
00:28:29,708 --> 00:28:30,608
Useless?

448
00:28:31,176 --> 00:28:34,112
Yes. For me...

449
00:28:34,179 --> 00:28:35,013
You are arrogant!

450
00:28:36,514 --> 00:28:37,716
You are arrogant!

451
00:28:39,984 --> 00:28:43,688
There are patients
who want to hear things again...

452
00:28:43,755 --> 00:28:45,657
no matter what the sound may be!

453
00:28:46,191 --> 00:28:49,360
I don't care what your job is.
You're just arrogant!

454
00:28:49,427 --> 00:28:50,895
- Dr. Kitano!
- Shut up!

455
00:28:50,962 --> 00:28:52,464
Did you just say that to me?

456
00:28:52,530 --> 00:28:54,165
- Hey. Hold on.
- What?

457
00:28:56,568 --> 00:28:57,402
Yuka.

458
00:28:59,037 --> 00:29:02,073
It's good that you visit here.

459
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:05,810
If you really don't want to receive
the surgery performed

460
00:29:05,877 --> 00:29:07,579
by Dr. Kitano,

461
00:29:07,645 --> 00:29:12,350
maybe Dr. Michiko Daimon...

462
00:29:12,417 --> 00:29:15,386
can take on it. What do you think?

463
00:29:15,453 --> 00:29:16,921
- What?
- What do you mean what?

464
00:29:17,455 --> 00:29:19,424
- You'll do it, right, Dr. Daimon?
- I decline.

465
00:29:19,491 --> 00:29:22,260
- Why not?
- It has nothing to do with me.

466
00:29:22,327 --> 00:29:23,762
What do you mean?

467
00:29:23,828 --> 00:29:25,497
Dr. Daimon, what are you doing?

468
00:29:25,563 --> 00:29:29,567
Still, with all my might,
I'll carry out her operation.

469
00:29:29,634 --> 00:29:30,702
- What?
- What?

470
00:29:30,769 --> 00:29:33,171
No way. It's Mr. Nanao
who needs it more than...

471
00:29:33,238 --> 00:29:35,507
Hey. At this rate,

472
00:29:35,573 --> 00:29:37,542
you will lose all hearing
in your right ear, too.

473
00:29:38,209 --> 00:29:39,043
What's worse,

474
00:29:39,577 --> 00:29:42,013
if you leave it untreated, you will die.

475
00:29:45,283 --> 00:29:46,284
What do you mean?

476
00:29:48,052 --> 00:29:52,423
The hearing in her left ear was lost
due to tympanitis during childhood.

477
00:29:53,124 --> 00:29:57,529
As resulting symptoms, the hearing
in her right ear is also being lost.

478
00:29:57,595 --> 00:30:00,765
Another symptom shown is facial pain.

479
00:30:00,832 --> 00:30:02,433
Facial pain?

480
00:30:02,500 --> 00:30:04,302
Does that mean...

481
00:30:05,303 --> 00:30:07,372
- it could be a brain tumour?
- Yes.

482
00:30:08,540 --> 00:30:09,474
A brain tumour?

483
00:30:11,810 --> 00:30:14,712
There's a large tumour in
the right cerebellopontine angle,

484
00:30:14,779 --> 00:30:17,182
pressing the right auditory nerves
and trigeminal nerve.

485
00:30:17,849 --> 00:30:20,185
Because of this, the hearing ability
in her right ear is degrading,

486
00:30:20,618 --> 00:30:22,187
causing trigeminal neuralgia.

487
00:30:22,620 --> 00:30:24,389
Without removing the tumour,

488
00:30:25,123 --> 00:30:26,591
the pressure on the medulla will worsen,

489
00:30:27,225 --> 00:30:28,893
so you will not only lose your hearing...

490
00:30:29,828 --> 00:30:30,995
but also die in the end.

491
00:30:38,303 --> 00:30:44,008
We all have the freedom to decide how
to live our own lives.

492
00:30:45,977 --> 00:30:50,248
Still, to make such decision,
we must be alive first.

493
00:30:56,187 --> 00:30:57,155
Therefore,

494
00:30:58,089 --> 00:31:00,525
I will start her
emergency operation tomorrow.

495
00:31:00,592 --> 00:31:01,826
Oh, sure.

496
00:31:08,333 --> 00:31:09,901
Prepare the spinal tap.

497
00:31:09,968 --> 00:31:10,835
Got it.

498
00:31:10,902 --> 00:31:12,604
- Please prepare more gauze.
- Okay.

499
00:31:40,031 --> 00:31:41,099
Is the anaesthesia ready?

500
00:31:41,165 --> 00:31:42,400
Already administered.

501
00:31:42,467 --> 00:31:45,770
Blood pressure, 105 and 70.
Heart rate is 62, normal.

502
00:31:46,971 --> 00:31:49,140
I will now begin to remove the
tumour in cerebellopontine angle.

503
00:31:49,207 --> 00:31:50,308
- Scalpel.
- Here you are.

504
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:03,454
- Bipolar.
- Here you are.

505
00:32:09,360 --> 00:32:14,132
I heard that Nanao refused
to receive the surgery.

506
00:32:14,832 --> 00:32:18,670
Well, maybe you are pretty lucky,
aren't you?

507
00:32:19,103 --> 00:32:20,872
Now, you don't have to worry about...

508
00:32:21,806 --> 00:32:23,408
making mistakes during the operation
you're not skilled.

509
00:32:24,275 --> 00:32:26,110
You can save your face at least.

510
00:32:35,320 --> 00:32:36,387
- Monopolar.
- Here you are.

511
00:32:58,543 --> 00:32:59,577
Director,

512
00:32:59,644 --> 00:33:02,280
why did you give her the permission
to operate on this lady?

513
00:33:03,681 --> 00:33:05,216
Well, you know...

514
00:33:05,883 --> 00:33:09,620
the broker guy who always
brings melons, right?

515
00:33:10,121 --> 00:33:11,422
That guy said...

516
00:33:12,223 --> 00:33:16,194
he would give this kind of article
to the press.

517
00:33:19,063 --> 00:33:22,266
"A moving story, Director Hiruma
of Toutei University Hospital...

518
00:33:22,333 --> 00:33:23,401
uses his discretion generously."

519
00:33:23,468 --> 00:33:27,105
Use discretion generously?
I feel a bit bashful.

520
00:33:27,171 --> 00:33:28,873
"In order to make the woman
hear piano sounds,

521
00:33:28,940 --> 00:33:31,843
he provides surgery to her
free of charge."

522
00:33:31,909 --> 00:33:35,179
It is kind of embarrassing
since they wrote this moving story...

523
00:33:35,246 --> 00:33:37,382
but I guess it's okay.

524
00:33:37,849 --> 00:33:39,650
The melon this time
must be very expensive.

525
00:33:39,717 --> 00:33:41,853
Right. But it's okay.

526
00:33:42,487 --> 00:33:44,022
I'll eat the whole thing.

527
00:33:49,360 --> 00:33:51,062
Spray a little more water.

528
00:33:51,129 --> 00:33:51,963
Got it.

529
00:33:56,768 --> 00:33:58,469
The sigmoid sinus is confirmed,

530
00:33:59,303 --> 00:34:00,605
and drilling is complete.

531
00:34:01,472 --> 00:34:03,508
I'll incise her dura mater,
Scalpel number 15.

532
00:34:13,851 --> 00:34:15,620
Here you are. We have reached
the surface of the cerebellum.

533
00:34:16,921 --> 00:34:18,456
- Wait.
- This is bad.

534
00:34:19,190 --> 00:34:21,125
The cerebellar hemisphere is so swollen.

535
00:34:21,192 --> 00:34:23,061
We'll never be able to remove
the tumour like this.

536
00:34:23,594 --> 00:34:24,529
This is bad.

537
00:34:25,029 --> 00:34:26,330
The pressure on the brain stem is so heavy

538
00:34:26,397 --> 00:34:28,032
that might not be able to breathe again.

539
00:34:33,504 --> 00:34:36,107
Blood pressure is rising.
It's 220 and 140.

540
00:34:37,842 --> 00:34:38,910
What should we do?

541
00:34:38,976 --> 00:34:41,479
There's venous reflux
in the cerebellum's fossa.

542
00:34:41,546 --> 00:34:43,214
- Administer mannitol.
- Already on it.

543
00:34:51,155 --> 00:34:52,857
We need to open up. Bipolar.

544
00:34:52,924 --> 00:34:54,192
Are you serious?

545
00:34:54,258 --> 00:34:55,493
- Hurry up.
- Got it.

546
00:35:11,042 --> 00:35:11,876
This is no good.

547
00:35:12,510 --> 00:35:15,413
If they keep going, the cerebellum
will also be blocked off.

548
00:35:24,155 --> 00:35:27,191
I NEVER FAIL

549
00:35:34,899 --> 00:35:35,967
- Micro scissors.
- Here you are.

550
00:35:56,154 --> 00:35:58,956
First assistant,
cut the coagulated vessel.

551
00:35:59,023 --> 00:36:01,325
- Got it. Micro scissors.
- Here you are.

552
00:36:05,796 --> 00:36:06,998
Second assistant, cotton sheet.

553
00:36:07,064 --> 00:36:07,899
Here it is.

554
00:36:16,874 --> 00:36:18,876
The cerebellum is removed.

555
00:36:27,151 --> 00:36:29,120
Blood pressure has returned to 110.

556
00:36:29,654 --> 00:36:30,488
Thank goodness.

557
00:36:30,922 --> 00:36:32,523
We've finally reached the tumour.

558
00:36:33,457 --> 00:36:35,526
I will now begin to remove the tumour.

559
00:36:35,593 --> 00:36:37,061
- Micro dissector.
- Here you are.

560
00:36:53,277 --> 00:36:54,645
It's incredible.

561
00:36:54,712 --> 00:36:58,216
She removes only the minimum
parts of the cerebellum.

562
00:37:05,790 --> 00:37:08,826
- Finished.
- Vitals are 110 and 83.

563
00:37:08,893 --> 00:37:10,661
Heart rate is 75, normal.

564
00:37:18,135 --> 00:37:19,237
Let's play table tennis again.

565
00:37:20,304 --> 00:37:21,872
- Nice work.
- Nice work.

566
00:37:21,939 --> 00:37:23,207
Good work.

567
00:38:04,815 --> 00:38:05,916
So, how is it?

568
00:38:58,469 --> 00:39:02,840
DEAF PIANIST TAKASHI NANAO

569
00:39:43,247 --> 00:39:49,854
I know which notes I should hit and how to
hit them to make the sound I want.

570
00:40:06,771 --> 00:40:08,873
Although I cannot hear through my ears,

571
00:40:09,774 --> 00:40:13,978
inside my head, sounds are ringing.

572
00:40:26,257 --> 00:40:32,630
I know exactly what my performance
would sound to her.

573
00:40:52,917 --> 00:40:55,419
I know very well.

574
00:40:56,487 --> 00:41:00,191
My music that reaches her heart.

575
00:41:04,495 --> 00:41:05,529
That's...

576
00:41:07,631 --> 00:41:10,734
the priceless bliss for me
who has chosen...

577
00:41:12,002 --> 00:41:15,339
to live my life as a musician.

578
00:42:16,033 --> 00:42:18,469
Bravo...

579
00:43:04,214 --> 00:43:05,082
Thank you.

580
00:43:06,917 --> 00:43:07,851
You know...

581
00:43:09,320 --> 00:43:11,922
I could fix your hearing completely.

582
00:43:14,658 --> 00:43:17,394
I'm just a surgeon, but I do have pride.

583
00:43:21,465 --> 00:43:22,566
I guess I should mind my own business.

584
00:43:25,736 --> 00:43:28,172
Go on. Encore.

585
00:43:43,420 --> 00:43:46,724
<i>This is the story of</i>
<i>a lone wolf woman doctor.</i>

586
00:43:48,859 --> 00:43:51,895
<i>Despising the herd mentality,</i>
<i>authority and…</i>

587
00:43:52,563 --> 00:43:53,664
<i>institutional restraints.</i>

588
00:43:54,465 --> 00:43:57,568
<i>Her solo weapons are her license</i>

589
00:43:58,135 --> 00:43:59,336
<i>and the skills she has earned.</i>

590
00:44:01,905 --> 00:44:03,507
It looks delicious!

591
00:44:04,375 --> 00:44:06,610
<i>Surgeon Michiko Daimon.</i>

592
00:44:07,211 --> 00:44:08,178
<i>Also known as...</i>

593
00:44:08,746 --> 00:44:10,214
<i>Doctor-X.</i>

594
00:44:12,683 --> 00:44:14,151
It looks so good!

595
00:45:04,401 --> 00:45:05,869
It's a top secret.

596
00:45:05,936 --> 00:45:08,872
We must make sure not to leak
patients' personnel information.

597
00:45:08,939 --> 00:45:10,708
You have a half year at best
without operation.

598
00:45:10,774 --> 00:45:12,476
A highly confidential delivery?

599
00:45:12,543 --> 00:45:14,344
- No way, Yuta!
- Hey.

600
00:45:14,411 --> 00:45:18,515
Sir. Please come back.
